{"kind":"request","text":"codex: fourth review of this lane, at exact clean pushed head 53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18. Reporting artifact acb00817 at internal/kernel; it retires the 2eaf52bf one.\n\nThe contract break is undone. checkpointLimitWriter is back to its form on main, so a serialized blob may again use the whole 256 MiB that limits.md:170 promises, and a test pins that by marshalling past half the limit. The construction policy lives in a separate checkpointChunkWriter that only the cache builds. I checked limits.md at the line rather than take my agents word for it.\n\nYour three defects are answered. The clone now goes into storage sized exactly to the lengths the preflight measured, so the check bounds the allocation instead of trailing it, and the false comment is gone. The chunks slice metadata carries a flat per-slot charge applied before each chunk is built. The cleanup hole is closed at the write boundary rather than by call ordering: reserve returns the whole limit once the cache has failed, so nothing can write against accounting that fail released.\n\nTwo things I want you to weigh rather than inherit.\n\nOne mutation deliberately survives, and it is in the table. Reinstating Close() after fail() changes nothing now, because the write guard refuses regardless of when the close runs. I think that is the right shape — the invariant is enforced where writes happen, not by remembering an ordering — but it does mean the ordering itself is untested, and you may disagree.\n\nAnd the pre-clone refusal is witnessed by an allocation count, a ceiling of eight against three or four actual, with the mutation driving it to twenty-six. The margin is wide but it is the one assertion here that depends on standard-library allocation behaviour rather than on our code. If you would rather it were witnessed some other way, say so.\n\nGates run by me at this head: gofmt clean, vet clean, full go test ./... -count=1 green across thirty packages.\n\nYour stale 0d178fd4 is still the other live artifact at internal/kernel; naming or retiring it keeps this merge from leaving an orphan.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Re-review exact clean pushed head 53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:acb00817430ff0ed94a23050df25cc761552c644 at internal/kernel, against the conditions of bc311b36 and all findings of your ratified verdict 228e43b1. Record all three conclusions. Architecture: confirm the documented 256 MiB serialized checkpoint ceiling from docs/reference/limits.md:170 is restored, that checkpointLimitWriter matches its form on main, and that splitting the artifact bound from the construction bound leaves the kernel contract intact. Security: judge whether the peak now holds at every instant across the borrowed, owned-tail, streamed and marshal paths, whether cloneExact makes the preflight bound the allocation, whether the chunk-slot charge covers the growing slice metadata, and whether returning the whole limit as reserve on a failed cache closes the cleanup-write hole. Simplification: say whether two writer types, the slot-overhead constant, the two-stage refusal, or any test can be cut without weakening the conditions. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. If you judge your own stale internal/kernel artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9ddf3443317e4f7f6bbc28748bb239810d178fd4, describing c086ee1e, to be covered by this head, name it in the verdict artifact set or retire it yourself. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}